WEBVTT

00:00:00.290 --> 00:00:04.690
Every day, organizations generate
an absolute avalanche of information.

00:00:05.250 --> 00:00:09.130
It pours out of supply chains,
financial systems and consumer apps.

00:00:09.730 --> 00:00:12.890
Raw data on its own is effectively useless
without professionals equipped to

00:00:12.890 --> 00:00:16.490
wrangle, clean and make sense of it.

00:00:17.130 --> 00:00:20.050
You'll hear terms like big data
thrown around constantly.

00:00:20.760 --> 00:00:25.360
In the wild, information often arrives
as unstructured data,

00:00:25.360 --> 00:00:30.240
like sprawling text documents,
or semi-structured data,

00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:33.040
which might have some loose
organizational tags,

00:00:33.040 --> 00:00:37.440
but still requires heavy processing.
To build true analytical mastery,

00:00:37.440 --> 00:00:37.440
we have to start with a solid foundation.

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:41.200
That means our focus in this course
is strictly on large,

00:00:41.200 --> 00:00:46.900
structured datasets, carefully organized
into distinct rows and columns.

00:00:47.700 --> 00:00:49.820
This is what that data
looks like under the hood.

00:00:50.500 --> 00:00:55.020
Here we're using R to call the structure
function on a fuel consumption data frame.

00:00:55.540 --> 00:01:02.220
You can immediately see 16 distinct
variables, showing what we can analyze.

00:01:02.220 --> 00:01:06.240
But possessing this massive spreadsheet
doesn't automatically grant you answers.

00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:11.400
A skilled analyst must apply
mathematical models to extract meaning.

00:01:11.880 --> 00:01:14.320
Those rows and columns
are just digital noise.

00:01:15.280 --> 00:01:21.000
Before predicting what a customer
or market might do tomorrow,

00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:21.000
we have to figure out what they do now.

00:01:21.720 --> 00:01:27.560
The first step in professional analysis
is mapping the data with descriptive statistics.

00:01:28.340 --> 00:01:31.720
We start by locating the middle
of the dataset,

00:01:31.720 --> 00:01:35.180
calculating centrality measures:
mean, median and mode.

00:01:35.940 --> 00:01:40.960
From there, we measure variation.
Variance and standard deviation show

00:01:40.960 --> 00:01:46.780
how far data spreads from the central peak,
and how dispersed it really is.

00:01:47.740 --> 00:01:52.020
This density plot shows those concepts
applied to real-world vehicle data.

00:01:52.620 --> 00:01:57.240
Purple bars show the spread
of actual engine sizes,

00:01:57.240 --> 00:02:04.140
while the red curve is the best-fit
normal distribution, a baseline for comparison.

00:02:04.140 --> 00:02:08.260
You cannot build a reliable forecast
without understanding your starting point.

00:02:09.140 --> 00:02:14.820
Establishing this foundational shape
is a prerequisite for predictive modeling.

00:02:15.800 --> 00:02:18.440
This brings us to a major hurdle
in data science.

00:02:19.240 --> 00:02:21.280
This diagram illustrates
our core constraint.

00:02:21.760 --> 00:02:23.960
We rarely have access
to an entire population.

00:02:24.600 --> 00:02:29.440
Instead, we pull a smaller sample,
so our data contains inherent uncertainty.

00:02:30.160 --> 00:02:32.680
To deal with that,
we rely on inferential statistics.

00:02:33.480 --> 00:02:38.680
Using frameworks like the
Central Limit Theorem,

00:02:38.680 --> 00:02:44.100
we construct confidence intervals:
a calculated range of plausible values,
not a fragile guess.

00:02:45.140 --> 00:02:48.140
Modern analysts have
a powerful new toolkit, too.

00:02:48.740 --> 00:02:54.100
Instead of relying only on assumptions,
we can use bootstrapping.

00:02:54.820 --> 00:03:01.020
We repeatedly resample collected data
to simulate thousands of new samples.

00:03:01.740 --> 00:03:03.820
This readout shows
the scale of that process.

00:03:04.420 --> 00:03:09.120
By running this operation,
we've generated a new data frame

00:03:09.120 --> 00:03:13.100
with 10,000 variables in seconds,
drastically improving model accuracy.

00:03:14.060 --> 00:03:18.920
Anyone can enter numbers in a spreadsheet,
but rigorously quantifying and managing

00:03:18.920 --> 00:03:25.820
statistical uncertainty separates
a trustworthy analyst from a guesser.

00:03:26.500 --> 00:03:29.740
Once we trust our data,
we can map how things interact.

00:03:30.420 --> 00:03:32.820
That is where multivariable
linear regression comes in.

00:03:33.460 --> 00:03:38.580
It tracks how multiple factors
influence one outcome.

00:03:39.300 --> 00:03:44.510
This scatter plot maps a simple relationship:
combined fuel use below, engine size above.

00:03:45.030 --> 00:03:48.150
The blue regression line
represents our calculated baseline.

00:03:48.630 --> 00:03:51.430
But reality rarely involves
just two factors.

00:03:52.270 --> 00:03:56.000
In a multivariable model,
points are colored by a gradient

00:03:56.000 --> 00:03:59.110
representing a third variable:
CO2 emissions.

00:04:00.030 --> 00:04:04.710
Capturing these overlapping dynamics
lets us predict real-world outcomes.

00:04:05.640 --> 00:04:11.520
Businesses rely on these models because
nothing happens in isolation in the market.

00:04:12.760 --> 00:04:16.240
Getting the math to run is a milestone,
but it's only

00:04:16.240 --> 00:04:19.120
the midpoint of your job
as a data scientist.

00:04:20.080 --> 00:04:25.200
This is a standard R console output:
a linear model summary and ANOVA table.

00:04:25.920 --> 00:04:26.640
It is dense.

00:04:26.920 --> 00:04:29.440
It is loaded with p-values
and F-statistics.

00:04:29.760 --> 00:04:33.240
To a nontechnical executive,
it is completely unreadable.

00:04:34.160 --> 00:04:36.360
Your true professional objective
is translation.

00:04:37.000 --> 00:04:40.880
Take raw coefficients and standard errors,
then distill them

00:04:40.880 --> 00:04:45.720
into clear, actionable strategies
stakeholders can use to make decisions.

00:04:46.790 --> 00:04:52.400
Explaining complex behaviors, like how
multicollinearity distorts predictors,

00:04:52.400 --> 00:04:58.230
or how to build ggplot visualizations,
is expected in top-firm technical interviews.

00:04:59.190 --> 00:05:03.520
Knowing the equations might get you
a first-round interview,

00:05:03.520 --> 00:05:07.670
but explaining their impact on the bottom line
is what gets you the job.

00:05:08.560 --> 00:05:13.400
Stepping into advanced programming
and statistics involves a steep learning curve.

00:05:13.960 --> 00:05:16.640
The language is unfamiliar
and the concepts are dense.

00:05:17.200 --> 00:05:19.640
But it is an entirely manageable process.

00:05:20.480 --> 00:05:24.680
The ANA 605 course curriculum
supports you through every stage.

00:05:25.360 --> 00:05:31.770
We break complex mathematics into
clear, step-by-step applications,
so you never untangle the logic alone.

00:05:32.610 --> 00:05:37.610
Mastering these statistical tools
gives you confidence to lead with data.

00:05:38.410 --> 00:05:44.290
You'll uncover the story hidden in the numbers
and build a lasting, successful career.